# What the study r1/web-numeric-root found: prior art for one numeric node space under every language.

n: 4.4
name: numeric-root
kind: hub
is: Prior art for one numeric node space under every language with symbols as a lens. Content identity with names kept outside is in production (Unison 1.0, November 2025, after seven years). Label lenses work for a system's own vocabulary and stop at user-chosen names and at the way back from text. Stores shared across languages keep constructs per language. Natural language works in controlled fragments (46 languages, 3 to 9 months each); the open-domain attempt is disputed in 2026. One absent marker with several readings is the known failure.
limit: The study read every web page through a summarising fetch tool, nine fetches failed (among them Babelfish UAST, Dion and the SCIP design notes), long pages were read in their first 100,000 characters, and nothing was built or run. The encoder matched the wording of each web observation kept here against the page text on 2026-11-24.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.1
name: code-prior-art
kind: hub
is: Programming-side systems. NUMS.Go keeps symbols beside numeric nodes. Unison hashes name-free syntax trees and reached 1.0 after seven years. Label lenses stop at names the user chose. Code stores shared across languages keep a schema per language. Darklang abandoned its structure editor. Nix separates what was asked from what came out.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.1.1
name: nums-go-symbols-beside-nodes
kind: observation
is: NUMS.Go keeps a symbol table per recipe: seven maps from strings to node instances (locals, parameters, integers, decimals, strings, literals, labels) beside three maps keyed by node instance or blueprint id. Its HTTP model imports a tree-sitter binding and switches over twelve language ids to pick a default file extension.
status: observed
src: 2.14.2#9-15 2.14.3#10 2.14.3#15-43
limit: The first-round critic found 13 language ids in nums/parser.go and parser handlers for Go only; by the critic's verdict NUMS.Go represents a Go subset, executes nothing and does not build as checked in.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.1.2
name: unison-hash-excludes-names
kind: observation
is: Unison identifies each definition by a 512-bit SHA3 hash of its syntax tree, excluding all names; names are separately stored metadata that do not affect the hash. A member of a mutually recursive cycle is addressed by the cycle's hash plus an index, the cycle being ordered by sorting its members by their individual hashes.
status: observed
src: 2.14.4 2.14.5
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.1.3
name: unison-seven-years-to-1-0
kind: observation
is: Unison's milestone list gives the company founding in February 2018, a first alpha in August 2019, a move in April 2021 from a git-style filesystem codebase to SQLite for a 100x size reduction, and the 1.0 release in November 2025. C FFI support is listed among work still ahead.
status: observed
src: 2.14.6
limit: A second store-format change, in version 1.1.0 of January 2026, reached the study only as a search-result summary of a blog post that was not fetched.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.1.4
name: numbering-is-arbitrary
kind: observation
is: The Stanford Encyclopedia entry on the incompleteness theorems says of arithmetisation that there are many possible ways of accomplishing it and the details do not really matter. Of the diagonal construction it says the two sides have the same truth value and that no sameness of meaning is claimed.
status: observed
src: 2.14.7
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.1.5
name: binder-names-removed-by-indices
kind: observation
is: Terms written with de Bruijn indices are invariant under alpha-conversion, so alpha-equivalence is syntactic equality. The locally nameless form keeps indices for bound variables and names for free ones and gives a unique representation of terms. The Dhall standard's alpha-normalisation renames all bound variables to de Bruijn indices.
status: observed
src: 2.14.8 2.14.9#1-2 2.14.10
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.1.6
name: label-lens-stops-at-user-names
kind: observation
is: Excel translates function names for display each time a file is opened, and quoted text inside a formula is not translated. Changing the Scratch editor language does not translate variable names. Wikifunctions shows labels in more than 300 languages; its labelized view is often ambiguous and cannot always be translated to the machine-readable form.
status: observed
src: 2.14.11 2.14.12 2.14.13
limit: The Excel source is a secondary site, not the vendor. The study quoted the Scratch and Wikifunctions sentences in the fetch tool's paraphrase; the wording here follows the pages.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.1.7
name: shared-syntax-types-replaced
kind: observation
is: GitHub's semantic project replaced hand-written syntax types shared across languages, which its documentation calls essentially untyped, with trees generated per language from each tree-sitter grammar; the repository was archived on 2025-04-01. CodeQL has a database schema per language; Glean allows language-specific detail in each schema.
status: observed
src: 2.14.15 2.14.16 2.14.17 2.14.18
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.1.8
name: mlir-generic-form-and-k-archive
kind: observation
is: MLIR's rationale describes pluggable dialects, progressive lowering, and a generic textual form that is a safe fallback beside each dialect's custom form. The K framework lists semantics for C, WebAssembly and EVM as current and for LLVM (2011-2018), Python (2012-2013), Java (2012-2016), JavaScript (2013-2015) and x86-64 (2017-2020) as archived.
status: observed
src: 2.14.19 2.14.20
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.1.9
name: interaction-cores-are-deterministic
kind: observation
is: Interaction combinators have three agents (eraser, duplicator, constructor) and can simulate any other interaction system; strong confluence means no synchronisation is needed. Interaction nets are essentially deterministic and cannot model non-deterministic computation directly. The Bend 2 README says Bend 1 programs and HVM do not carry over.
status: observed
src: 2.14.21 2.14.22 2.14.43#288 2.14.43#526-530
limit: The study's HVM1 figures (speed against GHC, a production target of Q1 2024) stand in the README of the HVM repository's default branch, master, and are left out of this node by choice. The address the study cites now redirects to HVM1; only the README on branch main, which describes HVM2, lacks them.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.1.10
name: structure-editors-and-text
kind: observation
is: Darklang's post of 2024-03-12 reports abandoning its structural editor: about 50% of the codebase, buggy, and apart from users' normal development flows; a language server and existing editors replace it. Hazel typechecks and runs incomplete programs through holes, so no editor state is meaningless. MPS needs special support for diff and merge.
status: observed
src: 2.14.23 2.14.24 2.14.14
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.1.11
name: nix-two-output-addresses
kind: observation
is: In Nix an input-addressed output path changes whenever the derivation that asks for it changes, while a content-addressed output path depends only on the name, the data and the addressing method, so a change with no real effect forces no massive rebuilds. A self-referring output needs a provisional path, a choice the manual likens to an impurity.
status: observed
src: 2.14.25
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.2
name: language-prior-art
kind: hub
is: Natural-language systems. Grammatical Framework renders one tree in 46 languages at 3 to 9 months each. Wikidata and Wikifunctions key things and functions by number with labels per language. Abstract Wikipedia faces a request to pause in 2026. AMR says it is not an interlingua. Montague semantics treats meaning as a homomorphism from syntax.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.2.1
name: gf-abstract-and-concrete-syntax
kind: observation
is: In Grammatical Framework an abstract syntax defines what meanings can be expressed and a concrete syntax per language maps them to strings; parsing takes a string to an abstract tree. Version 3.12 is dated 2025-08-08. Its resource library lists 46 languages, some partial; adding one takes 3 to 9 months; translation is usually for limited fragments.
status: observed
src: 2.14.26 2.14.27
limit: The count of 46 is a count of the names in the page's list; the page states no number.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.2.2
name: wikidata-wikifunctions-numbered-labels
kind: observation
is: Wikidata identifies an item by Q and a number and gives it a label, a description and any number of aliases. Wikifunctions holds ZObjects, which its function model calls basically abstract syntax trees, with labels in more than 300 languages; all its functions are pure. A status headline of 2026-09-03 reads 5,000 Functions.
status: observed
src: 2.14.28 2.14.13 2.14.29 2.14.30
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.2.3
name: abstract-wikipedia-disputed-2026
kind: observation
is: Abstract Wikipedia was approved in May 2020 and is in soft launch in 2026 with three pilots. Google.org fellows advised decoupling it from Wikifunctions. A request for comment of 2026-07-14 reports that of 13 random abstract pages none was an acceptable article and proposes pausing or closing; the team replied 2026-08-20 that it is early beta.
status: observed
src: 2.14.31 2.14.32 2.14.33
limit: The request for comment is a live dispute, an opening statement by one editor plus replies, not a neutral evaluation; the study read the first 200,000 characters of it. The year 2022 for the fellows' evaluation is the study's and is not in the page text.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.2.4
name: meaning-representations-and-interlingua
kind: observation
is: The AMR 1.2.6 specification (2019-05-01) says AMR is geared toward English and is not an interlingua. UMR 0.9 (2022-08-08) is meant to be cross-lingual: it structures annotation domains as lattices and uses general roles such as actor and undergoer. Universal Dependencies release 2.19 (2026-11-15) has over 200 treebanks in over 150 languages.
status: observed
src: 2.14.34 2.14.35 2.14.36
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.2.5
name: designed-universals
kind: observation
is: Montague semantics holds that natural and formal languages differ in no theoretically important way; the Stanford Encyclopedia sums it up as syntax is an algebra, semantics is an algebra, and meaning is a homomorphism between them. Lojban aims to be syntactically unambiguous. Natural Semantic Metalanguage has 65 primes (14 in 1972).
status: observed
src: 2.14.39 2.14.37 2.14.38
limit: The limits of Montague semantics that the study lists (indexicals and context, hyperintensionality, cross-sentence anaphora, idioms) were read through the fetch tool only.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.3
name: absent-value
kind: hub
is: How a third, absent value has been read. Logic gives it at least four readings: undefined output, modal possibility, a junk value, and neither-or-both. SQL's single NULL carries both missing and inapplicable and is called inherently flawed. CUE puts every value in one lattice with a top and a bottom and merges independently of order.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.3.1
name: third-value-has-several-readings
kind: observation
is: The Stanford Encyclopedia entry on many-valued logic (2026-01-17, revised 2026-09-29) records readings of a third truth value: the undefined output of a partial recursive function (Kleene), modal possibility (Lukasiewicz, who doubted bivalence over future contingents), a non-sensical junk value, and neither true nor false or both true and false.
status: observed
src: 2.14.40
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.3.2
name: sql-null-one-marker-two-readings
kind: observation
is: SQL has one NULL marker for both missing and inapplicable information, and comparisons with it yield Unknown. A 1990 book by the relational model's author called the single marker inadequate and proposed two markers that say why data is missing. Two other authors call the SQL NULL implementation inherently flawed.
status: observed
src: 2.14.41
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.3.3
name: cue-one-lattice
kind: observation
is: In CUE types and values sit in one lattice with a top and a bottom: bool is as much a value as true and false, unification is the greatest lower bound, and bottom, the result of a conflict such as true with false, is an instance of every value. Two configurations merge to the same result independently of order.
status: observed
src: 2.14.42
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.4
name: verdicts
kind: hub
is: What the study concluded. The thesis splits into six claims with uneven support. Three counter-arguments stand: a numeric root is trivial or unproven, natural language is no lens over a fixed meaning space, and leaving text has cost more than it returned. A run has a request identity and a result identity; concepts need a lattice of granularity.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.4.1
name: six-claims-uneven-support
kind: finding
is: Six claims: numeric encoding is settled and uninformative; content identity with free names holds for pure code; symbol lenses are proven only for a system's own vocabulary; many languages share one node space structurally, not semantically; natural language holds in controlled fragments; three states suffice if readings of absence stay apart.
status: inferred
conf: mid
from: 4.4.1.2 4.4.1.4 4.4.1.6 4.4.1.7 4.4.2.1 4.4.2.3 4.4.3.2
src: 2.14.1#45-52
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.4.2
name: counter-numeric-root-trivial-or-unproven
kind: finding
is: First counter-argument: any formal object can be numbered, arbitrarily, so a numeric root says nothing until equal meanings get equal or relatable identities across languages. Shared construct sets failed in practice, and a content hash tells apart programs that behave alike. Content identity and vocabulary lenses still stand.
status: inferred
conf: mid
from: 4.4.1.4 4.4.1.7 4.4.1.8 4.4.1.1
src: 2.14.1#625-633
limit: The study's sentence that NUMS.Go works for Go is corrected by the first-round critic: NUMS.Go represents a Go subset and executes nothing.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.4.3
name: counter-language-is-not-a-lens
kind: finding
is: Second counter-argument: natural language is not a lens over a fixed meaning space. AMR disclaims being an interlingua, UMR gave up one fixed granularity, Lojban removed only syntactic ambiguity, and Abstract Wikipedia faces a request to pause; meaning in use depends on speaker, time and discourse. Controlled-fragment rendering still stands.
status: inferred
conf: mid
from: 4.4.2.3 4.4.2.4 4.4.2.5 4.4.2.1
src: 2.14.1#635-644
limit: That learned models translate well with no explicit interlingua is from model memory, unverified. The old body's own two-tongue files (form/form-stdlib/nl-translate.fk and others) were opened by no first-round report (critic G3), so this side rests on prior art alone.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.4.4
name: counter-leaving-text-costs
kind: finding
is: Third counter-argument: leaving text and names has cost more than it returned. Unison needed seven years to 1.0 and a new store format and still lists a C FFI as future work; Darklang abandoned an editor that was half its code. The core idea worked early each time; the time went into the edge with the existing world. Hashing every node costs CPU.
status: inferred
conf: mid
from: 4.4.1.3 4.4.1.10 4.4.1.9 4.4.2.1
src: 2.14.1#560-566 2.14.1#646-655
limit: No hashing cost was measured by the study.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.4.5
name: request-and-result-identities
kind: finding
is: A recipe run has two identities worth keeping: the identity of the recipe with its arguments (what was asked) and the identity of the result (what came out). Only the second lets an unchanged result cut recomputation short. A cell that contains its own address is the hard case and needs one rule fixed early.
status: inferred
conf: mid
from: 4.4.1.11 4.4.1.2 1.3
src: 2.14.1#329-332 2.14.1#609-610
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.4.6
name: concept-lattice-and-context-cells
kind: finding
is: For natural-language lenses, concept nodes sit in a lattice of granularity: a language attaches words at the level it lexicalises, and crossing lenses may generalise, with the loss recorded. Speaker, time, discourse and locale are argument cells of the offer and never part of the sentence node.
status: inferred
conf: low
from: 4.4.2.4 4.4.2.5 4.4.3.3 1.5
src: 2.14.1#595-600
limit: No system that builds such a lattice for a kernel was examined; the proposal is drawn from annotation guidelines.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.8
name: keep
kind: hub
is: What the study recommends carrying forward: identity as a hash of name-free structure; names in a table per lens; a lens as a total linearise and a choice-valued parse; one grammar with namespaces of blueprints; host languages as namespaces; three identities kept apart; holes as nothing; one nothing with one propagation rule; four separable stages.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.8.1
name: hash-name-free-structure
kind: finding
is: Identity is a hash of the name-free structure: binders by index or level, free references by node id, names excluded. Hash the structure, not a normal form, and record that two blueprints behave the same as an observed relation between two ids. Fix the rule for cycles and self-reference in the first design round.
status: inferred
conf: high
from: 4.4.1.2 4.4.1.5 1.3
src: 2.14.1#122-127 2.14.1#572-575
limit: Hash width and the in-memory handle are unmeasured. Whether literal values sit inside the hashed structure is not chosen (critic C7), and other first-round reports keep a four-field coordinate as the id (critic C2).
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.8.2
name: names-per-lens-and-lens-as-recipes
kind: finding
is: Names live in a side table keyed by node id and lens id; a lens id is itself a node, and user-chosen names are entries too. A lens is a pair of recipes in the one grammar: linearise is total, parse is a choice that may yield several candidate nodes or nothing. A label table alone is the lens that suffices for keywords and opcodes.
status: inferred
conf: high
from: 4.4.1.1 4.4.1.6 4.4.2.1 1.7.2
src: 2.14.1#544-551 2.14.1#577-582
limit: No deployed label-lens system translates user-chosen names, and every one the study examined uses assigned ids, not content hashes; a content-addressed store with a multilingual name lens has no precedent among the systems read.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.8.3
name: one-grammar-with-namespaces
kind: finding
is: One grammar after the MLIR pattern: one node shape, one generic printed form that always round-trips, and namespaces of blueprints. What BML adds over Form (choice, fail, stop, store, templates, generics, inheritance, objects) becomes higher-level blueprints that lower to core ones in the same node space; surface sugar is a lens.
status: inferred
conf: mid
from: 4.4.1.8
src: 2.14.1#584-586
limit: That MLIR has one node shape (operation, operands, regions, attributes) is the study's reading of the rationale page, not a sentence matched in it. The list of what BML adds over Form was verified by nothing this study opened (locally the axioms file and two NUMS.Go files); the critic found no run of two items the request calls proven, multiple inheritance and objects as data pointer plus interface pointer.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.8.4
name: host-languages-as-namespaces
kind: finding
is: Each host language (Go, C, Python) enters as its own namespace of precise blueprints, with tree-sitter in front as in NUMS.Go. Cross-language equivalences are recorded as explicit, witnessed relation cells. Host toolchains are reached through offered interfaces.
status: inferred
conf: high
from: 4.4.1.7 4.4.1.8 4.4.1.1 1.4
src: 2.14.1#553-558 2.14.1#588-590
limit: The old body's own host-language grammars (form/form-stdlib/grammars/go-bmf.fk and four others) were opened by no first-round report (critic G4).
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.8.5
name: three-identities-kept-apart
kind: finding
is: Three identities stay distinct: the blueprint (a content hash), the named cell (a pointer that moves; undo is repointing) and the external coordinate (a Wikidata id, a path in a repository). An external id is a name on a node, never its identity. Open numeric vocabularies such as Wikidata's Q, P and L ids are borrowed as names, not minted again.
status: inferred
conf: high
from: 4.4.1.2 4.4.2.2 1.3 1.7.1 1.7.8
src: 2.14.1#530-542 2.14.1#592-593 2.14.1#620-621
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.8.6
name: holes-are-nothing-text-stays
kind: finding
is: Incomplete input is first class: a hole is nothing in a typed position, and every state of the node space can be printed, hashed and offered. Text stays a full editing lens, with a total printer and a partial parser, because Darklang abandoned a tree editable only through its own editor; MPS goes on with special tools for diff and merge.
status: inferred
conf: mid
from: 4.4.1.10 1.1
src: 2.14.1#312-317 2.14.1#602-603
limit: One abandonment is the evidence, not a rule: the study's general sentence that a tree editable only through a bespoke editor fails is its own inference, and the MPS 2026.2 help, whose concrete syntax is not pure text, still describes diff and merge handled inside the tool.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r1/web-numeric-root

n: 4.4.8.7
name: one-nothing-one-rule-reasons-beside
kind: finding
is: Keep the state set at three, with one nothing and one propagation rule for every operator. Carry the reason for a nothing (timeout, declined, unreferenced, held) as an optional observable cell beside the state, as the absence-is-not-a-verdict theorem already does. Decide whether nothing is the lattice top and fail the lattice bottom.
status: inferred
conf: mid
from: 4.4.3.1 4.4.3.2 4.4.3.3 1.1 1.7.17 1.7.19
src: 2.14.1#508-526 2.14.1#605-607
